From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000013 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jason Gross <jason@theorem.dev>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 06:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tcc: parse floating-point literals with pure integer
 arithmetic (no ldexp/strtod)

parse_number() built the value of a floating constant in `double`: the
hex-float path finished with `d = ldexp(d, exp_val - frac_bits)` and the
decimal path called strtof/strtod/strtold. Both depend on the tcc
binary's own floating-point support and its linked libc. When tcc is
built by a compiler/libc without working floating point -- MesCC, which
compiles every `double`/`float` as a 32-bit int, over mes libc whose
ldexp is a `return 0;` stub and whose strtod backend is wrong -- every FP
literal is destroyed: `0x1pN` parses to 0 (ldexp stub), the hex mantissa
reconstruction `(double)bn[1]*4294967296.0` truncates 2^32 to 0, and the
decimal path returns garbage. Such a tcc then bakes zeroed constants into
musl's scalbn.o/floatscan.o when it compiles musl, poisoning the very
strtod a later tcc would link -- a circular dependency no tcc in the
lineage can break.

Rewrite the literal-to-bits step to construct the IEEE-754 encoding with
integer arithmetic only -- no FP op, no 64-bit type, no multiply (x10 is
(x<<3)+(x<<1)) -- so it is correct even when tcc itself has no working
floating point. The value is formed as an exact rational N/D of fixed-size
uint32-limb bignums (hex M*2^be, or decimal significand*10^e10), then
divided and rounded to the target format with round-to-nearest-even;
subnormals, overflow->inf and underflow->0 are handled. The result words
are written through the CValue integer view (tokc.tab[]) rather than
tokc.d/f/ld, so the bits never transit an FP register or op (this pairs
with the init_putv tab[] bit-copy of patches 0011/0012).

Scope: float and double are converted on every target (the routine is
correctly rounded, i.e. bit-identical to a conforming strtod, so it does
not perturb any existing self-host fixpoint). long double uses the integer
path only where long double == double 8 bytes: TCC_TARGET_PE and
TCC_TARGET_ARM (ARM EABI). Wider long double (x86 80-bit, arm64/riscv64
128-bit) keeps the historical ldexp/strtold reconstruction unchanged --
LDOUBLE_SIZE is not visible in tccpp.c (included before the *-gen.c that
defines it), which is why the gate is on TCC_TARGET_ARM, not LDOUBLE_SIZE.

bignum sizing (BIG_N) exceeds the largest intermediate reachable from a
token_buf-bounded (STRING_MAX_SIZE) literal, so no valid literal can
overflow it. Validated: vs glibc strtod/strtof the routine is exact on a
historical-killer battery, on 400k random digit strings, and on 8,000,000
random IEEE bit patterns round-tripped through excess-precision decimal
(3M double + 3M float + 2M hex); and MesCC compiles the routine to
byte-identical output as gcc (zero VFP mnemonics emitted).
